WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:04.740
we help a cloudy developer
get clarity in the tracing

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.210
and high instance counts.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.160
Does the forecast
call for sunny skies?

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.400
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.530
An old friend of mine recently
launched a new image service

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.050
called Partly Cloudy which
provides high resolution

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.270
images of clouds for
developers who need to use them

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.860
in professional presentations.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.570
After a week of
launching her open beta,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.090
she woke up one morning to
see this little gem waiting

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.265
for her in the Google
Cloud developer console.

00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:28.140
The blue line in this
graph charts the number

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.350
of active App Engine instances
that the application is using.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.390
These numbers are
really high, considering

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.910
the application's only
job is to grab an image

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.170
and send it to the user.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.420
But the developer of
Partly Cloudy had a hunch.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.290
She was seeing really
high latency numbers

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.200
in the trace results
and was assuming

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.990
that this was responsible for
the high number of instances.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.450
In some cases,
high response time

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.820
can cause new instances
to be spun up.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.760
So after looking at this list
of traces, I had to agree.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.480
This looked like
the obvious problem.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.020
However, when I dug into
the tracing information,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.600
things started to
get really weird.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.130
The latency was being
reported as six seconds,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.190
but the trace information said
that the sum of all RPC calls

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.630
was only taking about
180 milliseconds.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.560
Now, while digging in more,
things started to get wacky.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.050
I added some custom tracing
code around the entire response

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.508
section, which
showed us that there

00:01:14.508 --> 00:01:16.590
was no rogue code executing
and taking up time.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.085
Things were happening
really fast.

00:01:18.085 --> 00:01:19.710
The big issue here,
though, was that we

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.350
weren't seeing the huge six
second delays that the logs

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.570
were showing from before.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.860
In fact, any repo
I tried didn't seem

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.350
to exhibit the same slow
performance numbers, even when

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.972
submitting the exact
same HTTP request.

00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:32.430
The breakthrough
came when I looked

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.160
at the logging information
for the slower request.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.180
It was coming from
a mobile device.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.290
This gave me an idea.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.710
What if the connection speed
was causing the trace numbers

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:41.557
to be inflated?

00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:43.890
To test this theory out, I
throttled my connection speed

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.020
with a few curl commands,
which you can also

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.170
do in Chrome Dev Tools, and
got the following timings.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.012
Sadly, still no changes.

00:01:51.012 --> 00:01:53.220
When dealing with really
odd discrepancies like this,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.750
it's sometimes good
to take a break.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.100
So we grabbed some tacos.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.964
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:58.964 --> 00:02:01.130
When we came back, we found
that our quizzical error

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.080
had gotten even more complex.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.360
The traces, which had
low latency before,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.862
suddenly had a higher
value in the tracing list.

00:02:07.862 --> 00:02:09.320
After another 30
minutes, it seemed

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.570
that the graph had updated
with these new numbers.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:13.160
Then I remembered something.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.230
Sitting between user connections
and your App Engine instance

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.140
is a series of App
Engine front ends.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.590
These front ends will act as
a liaison between the client

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:21.650
and the given instance.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.380
When the App Engine instance
is done processing a request,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.360
the data is passed back
to the front end, which

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.460
stays connected to the
client as long as it

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.120
takes to transfer the data.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.120
While this is happening,
the App Engine instance

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.890
can be off servicing
other requests.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.650
Now what this means to you is
that regardless of how slow

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.270
the client connection
is, it's not

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.280
going to impact
your instance count.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.810
Now, it makes sense
then that there

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.260
will be a slight delay in the
traces receiving and updating

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.280
this information.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.752
There's lots of
complex steps here.

00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:50.210
And all that data has to be
gathered and presented properly

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.650
to the tracing tool.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.110
More importantly, this means
that Partly Cloudy's instance

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.930
count was not a function of
connection speed or weird code

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:57.650
issues.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.750
Turns out, the reason for
the high instance count

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.340
was resounding success.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.440
When we flipped over
to the traffic graph,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.120
we saw that over
the past six hours,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.610
people have been using the
Partly Cloudy service really

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:08.390
aggressively.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.970
Partly Cloudy was a
victim of its own success.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.720
But still, the
instances are too high.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.320
And now that we knew that there
wasn't some weird code issue,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.330
we could get to bringing
that number down.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.250
It turns out that Partly
Cloudy's architecture

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.660
was really part of the problem.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.790
In their setup, App Engine
will handle the request

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.130
from a client, do an
NDB query to the data

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.650
store, which contains info
on all the 20,000 images

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:28.347
available.

00:03:28.347 --> 00:03:29.930
From there, the
instance will randomly

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.440
pick one, fetch the full
image from the GCS bucket,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.150
send it along, back with
its details, to the client.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.320
The solution then
is straightforward.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.810
Let's find a way
to spend less time

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.210
having the clients talk to
App Engine and more time

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.850
having them ping GCS buckets
directly, which meant

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.770
changing the access pattern.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.930
When the client fetches
an image from GAE,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.860
append the next 10 images ID as
an extra header in the field.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.110
The client will then fetch
the next 10 images directly

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:57.202
from GCS rather than needing
to go through GAE for it.

00:03:57.202 --> 00:03:59.660
When we're out of pre-fetched
images, go back to the server

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:00.950
and grab the next batch.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.420
When we flipped
over to this code,

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.160
the results were
better than expected.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.990
An eight-hour run saw
instance count drop

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.204
from about 16 instances
on average to about 1.5.

00:04:09.204 --> 00:04:11.370
See, that's the trick with
performance improvements.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.745
You have to eliminate all the
possibilities before really

00:04:13.745 --> 00:04:14.856
understanding the problem.

00:04:14.856 --> 00:04:16.730
And to learn more about
all the possible ways

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.380
that perf can go
sideways, check out

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.529
the rest of the Cloud
Performance Atlas videos.

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.400
And remember, when it
comes to performance,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.600
every millisecond counts.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

